content.text:
The product page was waiting on `getInventory()` (which calls `connection()`) before rendering anything, so the title was blocked behind that async work.

The fix splits the page into a static shell and a streamed async section:

```1:24:app/product/page.tsx
import { getInventory } from '@/lib/data'
import { Suspense } from 'react'

async function InventoryDetails() {
  const inventory = await getInventory()

  return (
    <>
      <p>{inventory.count} in stock</p>
      <p>Price: ${inventory.price}</p>
    </>
  )
}

export default function ProductPage() {
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Premium Widget</h1>
      <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading inventory...</p>}>
        <InventoryDetails />
      </Suspense>
    </main>
  )
}
```

**What changed:** The title is in the main page component and renders immediately. Inventory fetching moved into `InventoryDetails`, wrapped in `<Suspense>`, so it streams in after navigation.

The build confirms this with partial prerendering (`◐ /product`) — static HTML for the title ships right away, and the inventory details follow when the server responds.

